      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.57,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.35,
            0.11
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exterior Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.12,
            0.848,
            0.177
          ],
          "content": "The angle formed between the extension of a side of a triangle and the other side is called an **exterior angle** of the triangle. In this figure, $\\angle  ACD$ is an exterior angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.187,
            0.674,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.351,
            0.521,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "Find $\\angle  ACD ,$ , if $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = 50^{\\circ}$ , and $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 60^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.382,
            0.614,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "From the angle sum property, we know that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.379,
            0.41,
            0.617,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "50^{\\circ}+60^{\\circ}+\\angle ACB=180^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.447,
            0.597,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "110^{\\circ}+\\angle ACB=180^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_21_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.471,
            0.328,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\angle ACB = 70^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.49,
            0.463,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\angle ACD = 180^{\\circ} - 70^{\\circ} = 110^{\\circ},$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.509,
            0.846,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": "since, $\\angle \\mathrm { A C B }$ and $\\angle  ACD$ together form a straight angle. Find the exterior angle for different measures of $\\angle \\mathrm { A }$ and $\\angle \\mathrm { B }$ . Do you see any relation between the exterior angle and these two angles? **[Hint:** From angle sum property, we have $\\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{B} + \\angle \\mathrm{ACB} = 180^{\\circ}.$ We also have $\\angle ACD + \\angle ACB = 180^{\\circ}$ , since they form a straight angle. What does this show?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.643,
            0.847,
            0.668
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.4 Constructions Related to Altitudes of Triangles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.677,
            0.847,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "There is another set of useful measurements with respect to a triangle the height of each of its vertices with respect to the opposite sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.715,
            0.574,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "In the world around us, we talk of the heights of various objects: the height of a person, the height of a tree, the height of a building, etc. What do we mean by the word ‘height’?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.806,
            0.572,
            0.861
          ],
          "content": "Consider a triangle ABC. What is the height of the vertex A from its opposite side BC, and how can it be measured?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.592,
            0.72,
            0.846,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.688,
                0.845,
                0.754,
                0.859
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 7.8"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.861,
            0.848,
            0.918
          ],
          "content": "Let AD be the line segment from A drawn perpendicular to BC. The length of AD is the height of the vertex A from BC. The line segment AD is said to be one of the ‘altitudes’ of the triangle. The other altitudes"
        },
        {
